# EL-2 — the *proxy's* half of an EchoLink proxy login and session lifecycle.
#
# Provenance (LP-1, `Tests/FIXTURES.md`): a capture of the maintainer's own
# EchoLink session, taken 2026-08-12, the work that advanced OQ-9. Nothing here
# comes from anyone else's implementation; these are the octets a real public
# proxy put on the wire in reply to us.
#
# The capture is cited by SHA-256 and never by path — see the last section of
# `Tests/FIXTURES.md`. It contains the operator's EchoLink account password in
# cleartext, in a type 0x02 frame in the *client* direction, so only the
# proxy's half is here and the client direction is not cut from this capture
# at all.
#
# The exchange, as it happened:
#
#   <==  8 ASCII hex bytes, unframed             the nonce ("6fc8b7e3")
#   ==>  callsign + LF + 16 raw digest bytes     omitted, see below
#   ==>  0x01 OPEN                               omitted (client half)
#   <==  0x04 STATUS  00 00 00 00                success
#   ==>  0x02 TCP DATA                           OMITTED — the account password
#   <==  0x02 TCP DATA  "OK"                     the directory server's reply
#   <==  0x03 CLOSE
#   ==>  0x01 OPEN                               omitted (client half)
#   <==  0x04 STATUS  00 00 00 00                success
#
# Three things this fixture pins that no hand-built one could:
#
#  * The login exchange is NOT framed. The nonce arrives as 8 bare ASCII bytes
#    before any 9-byte header appears, which is why a decoder that starts
#    looking for headers at byte 0 desynchronises on a capture that includes
#    the TCP handshake, and appears to work on one that does not.
#  * The length field is LITTLE-endian: a 4-byte payload reads `04000000`.
#    RFC 5456 and M17 are both big-endian, so this is the one length field in
#    the tree running the other way, and the one most likely to be "corrected".
#  * The peer IPv4 field is 0.0.0.0 on every directory and control frame. It is
#    only meaningful on the 0x05/0x06 channels.
#
# The frame at index [4] is deliberately absent and must stay absent: it is the
# directory login line, `'l'` + callsign + two separators + the operator's
# account password + CR, in cleartext. The `[n]` indices make the gap visible.
#
# The one type 0x02 frame that IS here, index [5], is the two bytes 4f 4b —
# "OK". It was cut with `--allow-tcp-data` after checking those exact two
# bytes; it carries no credential and no third-party data. EL-6 tests against
# it.
# Machine-generated. The captures themselves are not in the repository —
# they are the maintainer's own, and large. Regenerate with:
#   scripts/pcap-to-fixture.py <capture> --transport tcp --stream 1 --dir in --range 0:8 --allow-tcp-data
# Capture SHA-256: c66d11f9bce26158c8723c13c2846e62a2a35e040b929a16545f3f8433359ddf
#
# Each line is one EchoLink proxy frame verbatim, reassembled from the
# TCP stream: the 9-byte header — type(1), peer IPv4(4), length(4,
# LITTLE-endian) — and its payload. `[n]` is the frame's index in
# arrival order across both directions, so a gap in the indices is a
# frame deliberately left out.

# [0] 0.000s <== LOGIN        nonce 6fc8b7e3      8B
3666633862376533
# [3] 1.596s <== STATUS          4B  success
04 00000000 04000000 00000000
# [5] 2.184s <== TCP DATA        2B
02 00000000 02000000 4f4b
# [6] 2.523s <== CLOSE           0B
03 00000000 00000000
# [8] 3.128s <== STATUS          4B  success
04 00000000 04000000 00000000
